According their graphs, the one-sided limits at $x=-1$ are different for both functions and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1}f(x)$ and $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1}h(x)$ don't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1}\Big(f(x)-h(x)\Big)$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}f(x)=2}
\\\\
&\purpleD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^+}f(x)=1}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}h(x)=1}
\\\\
&\goldD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^+}h(x)=0}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1}\Big(f(x)-h(x)\Big)$ using the fact that the limit of a difference of functions is the difference of the individual limits:

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}\Big(f(x)-h(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to -1^-}f(x)-\lim_{x\to -1^-}h(x)
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^+}\Big(f(x)-h(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to -1^+}f(x)-\lim_{x\to -1^+}h(x)
\end{align}$
